<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liberal Revolution of 1820</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Liberal Revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Liberal Revolution of 1820 was a constitutionalist uprising in Portugal, beginning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a>. It demanded a constitution and the return of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> VI from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, where the court had lived since 1808. The king's return and Portuguese attempts to reduce Brazil's status led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grito-do-ipiranga">Brazilian independence</a> in 1822 and the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1820.jpg" alt="Liberal Revolution of 1820" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oscar Pereira da Silva · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sess%C3%A3o_das_Cortes_de_Lisboa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The context was the crisis produced by <b>Portugal's Liberal Revolution</b> of 1820, whose Cortes demanded the return of the royal court to Lisbon and the reduction of Brazil to its former colonial status — terms that Pedro, who had grown up in Brazil after the court's flight from…</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Called back to Lisbon by the <b>Liberal Revolution of 1820</b>, John VI left his heir, who refused the Cortes' demand to reduce Brazil to a colony on the Dia do Fico, 9 January 1822, and was crowned Emperor Pedro I after independence on 7 September 1822.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Liberal Revolution of 1820</b> brought constitutional rule, settled by the Constitutional Charter of 1826, which with amendments lasted until 1910.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Liberal Revolution</b> in Porto in 1820 forced John VI to return to Lisbon in 1821, leaving his son Pedro as regent.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves">United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves</a></li></ul></section>
